  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nPleasant weather conditions will prevail across South Texas today, \nwith mostly clear skies and light winds expected through much of the \nafternoon. Onshore flow will gradually return as winds shift toward \nthe east, allowing temperatures to climb into the mid to upper 70s \nacross the Coastal Bend and surrounding areas. \n\nA mid-level low is expected to approach the region Saturday morning, \nbringing increased cloud cover and a low to moderate (20-40%) chance \nfor isolated to scattered showers. Activity will generally progress \nfrom west to east, with localized higher precipitation expected \nacross the Coastal Plains and Victoria Crossroads as the disturbance \nmoves eastward. Following the passage of this disturbance, an \napproaching upper-level trough will support the progression of a \ncold front toward South Texas.\n\nAhead of the front, a southeasterly to easterly flow will allow for \nadditional moisture to progress into the region, supporting greater \nrain chances. GEFS guidance suggests PWAT values increasing to \naround 1.5-1.6\", slightly above the 75th percentile. Rain chances are \nexpected to increase compared to Saturday to around (50-90%), with \nscattered showers becoming more likely across the Coastal Plains, \nparticularly the Victoria Crossroads.\n\nBoth WPC and SPC have placed portions of South Texas under a \nMarginal Risk for excessive rainfall (Level 1 of 4) and severe \nthunderstorms (Level 1 of 5), respectively, for the period from 12Z \nSunday through 12Z Monday. The WPC Marginal Risk includes eastern \nportions of the CWA, including the Victoria Crossroads, where \nlocally heavy rainfall could lead to isolated instances of flash \nflooding. While  widespread severe weather is not \nanticipated, a few strong to severe thunderstorms will be possible \nSunday through Sunday night. Uncertainty remains regarding the \ntiming of the front and evolution of a surface low, which will \nultimately influence the location and magnitude of these threats.\n\nThere is a slight concern in the increase of minor coastal flooding \nwith a southeasterly flow that returns Sunday ahead of the front as \npreviously mentioned. Swell periods expected to increase to 7 to 8 \nseconds. Even though the winds will shift to be out of the northeast \nafter the front moves through, the periods still remain around 7-8. \nConclusively, the rip current risk will also increase, proceed with \ncaution for poor swimming conditions.\n\nAt this time, guidance is hinting at another possible front to move \nthrough our area sometime later next week, but being so far out, \nconfidence is very low at the moment. Will keep an eye on any \nupdates with that as the models update later into early next week.",
